{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: Site read-token reuse (R4 item 1) at exact head 63d056241aafef04bd8706f9b9da0edf7b3f35c6, tree de1e2eaa346f957a37cc4a3506769afa1769abd6, on builder/security-r4-site-token-reuse-20261025 (review request d9412f1f, which replaces ef483901; my promise ee717db5). This review binds child request a084acc9 (promise 2e1ab66c) to this one candidate; broader request 90878601 and the member quota stay open. My changes-requested report aa5caa8f on head 96bb50e6 had one blocking finding, no passing ordinary gate at the head's production bytes. That finding is resolved: gate 85802 passed at 96bb50e6 (tree e6540cbd), and this head differs from it only in the implementation note (+7/-4 lines, one path), so the gate stands under the documents-only rule. Every other verification from aa5caa8f carries unchanged because the 16 supporting sources are byte-identical to the gated head.\n\nThe gate record, verified: the raw record at /private/tmp/artroom-r4-site-token-readiness-gate-evidence-20261025-v1 has expectedHead 96bb50e6, expectedTree e6540cbd, unchangedCheckpoint true, gatePassed true, errors [], exit 0, and before.json identical to after.json. Its stdout shows eight stages at exit 0, \"Tests 3585 passed | 3 skipped (3588)\" over 500 passed and 1 skipped files, Node 42 and Counting 55. The copied typecheck log lists the workspace chains and the root chain; counted by invocation they total 40 tsc contexts with no TypeScript error. The independent audit at artroom-r4-site-token-readiness-gate-independent-audit-20261025-v1 records the same head, tree, stage exits, 40 contexts, totals, 17 exact stage copies, unique final IDs, zero unhandled errors, the five custody cases with complete ready, result and final records, and its 39 input pins all match the raw files on disk. The bound v2 index (37,115 bytes, SHA256 90083bf6…) verifies, and all 56 files it names exist with the stated sizes and digests; the note blob at the head is 10,418 bytes with the digest the index records (3c379715…). The 19 missing ready and 21 missing result callbacks have passing final records and are reported as a reporting gap, as before.\n\nHead checks: the branch's own worktree has moved to the builder's integration merge cec40d2f, so I signed from a detached worktree at the exact candidate; the candidate is 17 commits on Main 6c0a9a4d, 17 files, +645/-57; git diff --check is clean; check-commit-messages reports 17 checked, 0 flagged; the 17 artifact events at this head name exactly the 17 changed paths; the 16 non-note paths are byte-identical to 96bb50e6, and identical to the tested production checkpoint 81261e42df except the docs-only settings.md row from abdb70a8.\n\nLanding union: Main moved to 5a9aeaf5 (the owned-row archive bound) after this gate started. git merge-tree of 5a9aeaf5 and this head resolves without conflict to tree 99c05e0a, the same tree as the builder's integration commit. The union differs from this head only by the six archive paths landed in 5a9aeaf5, which were gated separately (gate 52302), and from Main only by the 17 R4 paths. The one shared file is the seal map, where the union keeps both the archive-driver slot (82da30f6…) and the four R4 slots. The two production changes are in disjoint modules, archive-adaptive, archive-driver and archive-frontier-cost on one side and site/host, site/route, site/token-reuse and the ScopeIndex object in worker.ts on the other. My own checks on an export of the union tree: scripts/security-entry-points.test.mjs passes 4 of 4 (1.29 s wall), and the three Scope compile contexts (tsconfig.json, tsconfig.test.json, test/support/tsconfig.node.json) exit 0 with TypeScript 7.0.2. No full gate has run on the union; on this evidence I judge the merge safe to land as the ordinary merge onto 5a9aeaf5.\n\nProduction correctness, from the code at the head (unchanged since aa5caa8f): token custody lives in the fixed ScopeIndex object behind a small port; acquire is binding-only and keyed by the current canonical configuration, the repository tuple and the ref, with fresh configuration, binding identity and provider-reported remote checks on every acquisition; one transaction reserves before any provider call under the caps of 256 keys and 32 reserved mints, with 8 followers per flight and 64 in total, refusing with capacity (503) when full; a reservation holds for start plus READ_TTL (120 s) plus 60 s and is released only when createToken was not entered, so an invalid or ambiguous answer after entry holds the key as ruling 3fdb03b9 permits; a stale late answer cannot install because the UPDATE is a compare-and-set on key, generation, reserved state and unexpired end; the token's end comes from the provider's expiresAt and is never extended; the alarm is only moved earlier and alarm() runs maintain then reschedules; an alarm failure after install clears the plaintext and keeps the known end; close drops the request-local transport and secrets without revoking; owner failures surface as the existing unavailable refusal (503) and provider step failures keep their step; the readiness callback reads the three settings afresh per call. The five custody cases, the site-route invariant change and the fixture Proxy that feeds the scripted provider to the owner are as reviewed. Docs: the three deployment tables and the SCOPE_INDEX setting row are consistent; no new setting, secret, binding or migration; the private table is created on first acquire.\n\nNonblocking notes, carried: a Site request can wait on the owner for up to the 180 s reservation deadline when the provider stalls; the two identical custody-clearing blocks in site-route.test.ts could be one helper. New: none.\n\nArchitecture: as before, custody in one module owned by the fixed object, the Site host asks and never mints, the route maps two refusal shapes, the test harness reaches the owner through a subclass and Proxy. The landing union adds nothing to this shape.\n\nSecurity: no new authority; read-scope tokens minted only after remote and configuration match; plaintext rests in the object's private SQL for at most the token's life, which the ruling accepts; no plaintext or provider payload in errors or logs; compare-and-set prevents stale installs; caps bound storage and concurrency; the acquire method is reachable only through the SCOPE_INDEX binding of the same Worker. The four seals reproduce and the inventory bijection passes at the head and at the union.\n\nSimplification: the duplicate clearing block in site-route.test.ts; nothing else.\n\nFindings: none blocking. Redeploy of the existing Workers after landing is planner-owned; no new settings.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:88f5d23e8d735ef9c74a823260644356ee15ab7f","binding":"assigned","head":"63d056241aafef04bd8706f9b9da0edf7b3f35c6","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:ee717db501b2caea1d879e2aaa47552ce76d2bd6\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:2e1ab66cff42d28de3fd9af422eab6d5c6ef4e0d\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:bf26ddc2cc5154fc14e72a2ccc175ebe2471faa9","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}